<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">
   <!--
     Suppression rationale (reviewed 2026-07-31):

     leaflet 1.9.4 / CVE-2025-69993 (bindPopup renders string content as HTML)

     The Leaflet maintainers' published position (github.com/Leaflet/Leaflet/issues/10214)
     is that this is intended, documented behavior of the raw bindPopup(string) API, not a
     library defect; callers are responsible for sanitizing untrusted input before passing
     it as an HTML string. No patched release exists or is planned in the 1.x line.

     Provenance never calls Leaflet's raw bindPopup(string)/setContent(string) API. The only
     Leaflet usage in this codebase is via `react-leaflet`
     (src/app/artworks/[id]/certificate/_components/scan-locations-map.tsx), which renders
     popup content as React children. React escapes all text content by default, so
     user-controlled scan metadata (city/country/timestamp) can never reach the DOM as raw,
     unescaped HTML. The XSS sink described in the advisory is not reachable from this
     application's code path.

     Re-evaluate this suppression if Leaflet is ever called directly with untrusted HTML
     strings (bindPopup/bindTooltip/setContent) outside of react-leaflet's component API.
   -->
   <suppress>
      <notes><![CDATA[
         False positive: application only uses leaflet via react-leaflet's <Popup> component
         API (React children, auto-escaped), never the raw bindPopup(string) HTML sink that
         CVE-2025-69993 describes. See docs/casa-evidence/dependency-scan-owasp.md for detail.
      ]]></notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/leaflet@.*$</packageUrl>
      <cve>CVE-2025-69993</cve>
   </suppress>

   <!--
     brace-expansion / GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (CVE-2026-14257)
     pnpm-lock.yaml resolves brace-expansion to 1.1.17 and 5.0.9, both of which are
     explicitly listed as patched by the upstream advisory (patched versions: 1.1.17,
     2.1.3, 3.0.3, 5.0.8+). Confirmed by direct lockfile inspection on 2026-07-31
     (`grep -n "^  brace-expansion@" pnpm-lock.yaml`). The Pnpm Audit Analyzer's local
     advisory match does not resolve the finding down to a specific installed version,
     so it continues to flag the package name generically even though both resolved
     versions are fixed. Revisit if `pnpm audit` or a future Dependency-Check release
     resolves this more precisely, or if brace-expansion is ever downgraded.
   -->
   <suppress>
      <notes><![CDATA[
         False positive: pnpm-lock.yaml pins brace-expansion to 1.1.17 and 5.0.9, both
         patched per the upstream advisory's own "Patched versions" list. See
         docs/casa-evidence/dependency-scan-owasp.md for verification steps.
      ]]></notes>
      <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:npm/brace\-expansion.*$</packageUrl>
      <vulnerabilityName>GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg</vulnerabilityName>
   </suppress>
</suppressions>
